298
00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:39.610
in Lancaster, Pennsylvania, who says,
为什么大家都不急着走？

299
00:15:40.190 --> 00:15:42.630
Why isn't everyone eager to leave?
为什么他们不收拾东西下班？

300
00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:45.030
Why aren't they packing up for the day?
为什么他们不收拾东西下班？

301
00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:46.830
Don't they want to go home?
他们不想回家吗？

302
00:15:47.050 --> 00:15:48.790
It seems odd for this bunch.
对这群人来说挺奇怪的。

303
00:15:49.350 --> 00:15:50.910
That's a great observation.
观察得很敏锐。

304
00:15:51.430 --> 00:15:53.290
Yeah, I mean, it's five to five.
是啊，我是说，现在都四点五十五了。

305
00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:54.790
I feel like...
我觉得……

306
00:15:55.610 --> 00:16:00.830
Listen, I know that we had to be annoyed by Andy singing, but I don't know.
听着，我知道我们得对安迪唱歌感到厌烦，但我不知道。

307
00:16:00.830 --> 00:16:04.970
I feel like this would trigger me to be happy because I'd be like, goodbye.
我觉得这反而会让我高兴，因为我会想，终于要下班了。

308
00:16:06.110 --> 00:16:06.310
Yeah.
是啊。

309
00:16:07.230 --> 00:16:10.810
I mean, your boss singing to you every day would get old.
我是说，老板每天对你唱歌确实会让人受不了。

310
00:16:11.090 --> 00:16:11.850
It would.
确实会。

311
00:16:14.050 --> 00:16:18.670
If you sang to me every day when we finished the podcast, if you were like,
如果你每天在我们录完播客时对我唱歌，如果你像这样说：

312
00:16:19.330 --> 00:16:20.890
Put the microphone away.
收起麦克风。

313
00:16:21.370 --> 00:16:22.830
Pack up your laptop.
收拾好你的笔记本电脑。

314
00:16:23.310 --> 00:16:24.670
I'd be like, oh my gosh.
我会觉得，天哪。

315
00:16:24.770 --> 00:16:25.670
Okay, we get it.
行了，我们知道了。

316
00:16:26.470 --> 00:16:31.450
Well, here's something I remember from shooting this, which is that I had closing time stuck in
嗯，关于拍摄这一幕我记得一件事，那就是《Closing Time》这首歌在我脑子里

317
00:16:31.450 --> 00:16:33.290
my head for the rest of the week.
盘旋了整整一周。

318
00:16:33.430 --> 00:16:34.710
Oh, for sure.
噢，肯定的。

319
00:16:34.950 --> 00:16:40.610
And after we rewatched this episode, it has been in my head all this week as well.
重温完这一集后，这首歌这周也一直在我脑子里挥之不去。

320
00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:43.430
So yes, I think it is maddening.
所以没错，我觉得这确实让人抓狂。

321
00:16:43.570 --> 00:16:48.090
I get why Dwight puts on a set of headphones to try to block it out.
我理解为什么德怀特要戴上耳机试图隔绝它。

322
00:16:48.290 --> 00:16:50.070
It makes sense to me in that way.
从这个角度看，这很合理。

323
00:16:50.070 --> 00:16:55.690
Pam really cracked me up in the scene because I felt like she was speaking to me.
帕姆在这一幕里真的把我逗乐了，因为我觉得她简直是在替我说话。

324
00:16:55.870 --> 00:16:59.170
I can hear a song over and over and over.
我可以一遍又一遍地听一首歌。

325
00:16:59.530 --> 00:17:01.450
I'll like retain four words.
但我只能记住四个词。

326
00:17:01.590 --> 00:17:02.830
Like I'm horrible.
我在这方面真的很差劲。

327
00:17:02.830 --> 00:17:05.750
I drive my husband crazy because he knows every word to every song.
我常把我丈夫逼疯，因为他记得每一首歌的每一个字。

328
00:17:05.910 --> 00:17:09.630
So when she was like, and home and home and home and home.
所以当她说“回家，回家，回家，回家”的时候。

329
00:17:09.630 --> 00:17:11.090
Yeah, exactly.
没错，就是这样。

330
00:17:12.650 --> 00:17:15.270
We had another fan question from Michelle W.
我们还有来自堪萨斯州曼哈顿的米歇尔·W的粉丝提问，她说：

331
00:17:15.370 --> 00:17:17.410
in Manhattan, Kansas, who said,
“我一直觉得了解剧集中某些歌曲的版权费用很有意思。”

332
00:17:17.490 --> 00:17:21.390
I always find it so interesting to see how much certain songs can cost in an episode.
“《Closing Time》花了多少钱？”

333
00:17:21.390 --> 00:17:23.650
How much did closing time cost?
嗯，米歇尔，你知道，没有《Closing Time》，这一幕根本没法演。

334
00:17:24.630 --> 00:17:28.290
Well, Michelle, you know, this scene really doesn't work without closing time.
它在我们的剧本初稿里就有。

335
00:17:28.290 --> 00:17:30.590
And this was in our table draft.
每一版剧本里都有它。

336
00:17:30.590 --> 00:17:32.410
It was in every draft of the script.
我们花了25000美元才拿到这首歌的版权。

337
00:17:32.550 --> 00:17:35.910
And we paid $25,000 to get this song.
史蒂夫·伯吉斯说，如今购买歌曲版权不像当年那么贵了。

338
00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:41.830
Steve Burgess said nowadays, getting song rights is not as expensive as it was back then.
在支付版权费方面，我们当时正处于行业的高峰期。

339
00:17:41.830 --> 00:17:46.370
We were kind of at the peak of the industry in terms of paying for rights.
但我开始思考这个问题。

340
00:17:46.910 --> 00:17:48.850
But I started thinking about this.
你知道，有时候我们为了在剧集里用一小段歌而支付的金额确实令人震惊。

341
00:17:49.010 --> 00:17:55.310
You know, it's been very shocking sometimes the amount of money we've paid to have a little bit of a song in an episode.
但当你考虑到这首歌现在已经被重播了多久？

342
00:17:55.550 --> 00:18:02.290
But when you think about the fact that that song has been replayed now for over what?
多少年了，在多少流媒体设备上，在Instagram上出现了多少次这样的片段？

343
00:18:02.290 --> 00:18:10.930
How many years, on how many streaming devices, on how many, like, little snippets on Instagram the scene or these songs has appeared?
这简直是捡了大便宜。

344
00:18:10.930 --> 00:18:12.290
It's like a bargain.
算下来每次播放才几分钱。

345
00:18:12.290 --> 00:18:13.830
It's like pennies per play.
可能每次播放还不到一分钱。

346
00:18:14.050 --> 00:18:16.150
It's probably fractions of pennies per play.
真的吗？

347
00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:17.230
Really?
就补偿艺术家的音乐创作及其对剧集的贡献而言。

348
00:18:17.550 --> 00:18:24.090
In terms of compensating the artist for their music and for their contribution to the episode.
是啊。

349
00:18:24.450 --> 00:18:25.070
Yeah.
我觉得我们赚到了。

350
00:18:25.350 --> 00:18:26.710
I think we got off cheap.
我觉得我们花得挺值的。

351
00:18:27.430 --> 00:18:28.070
Frankly.
坦白说。

352
00:18:28.070 --> 00:18:33.250
Well, like you said, when you think about how many times, it's just played over and over and over and over.
嗯，就像你说的，当你想到它被反复播放了多少次时，真的就是一遍又一遍又一遍。

353
00:18:33.670 --> 00:18:33.950
Yeah.
没错。

354
00:18:34.930 --> 00:18:37.690
Finally, this is a catch from Mackenzie in Utah.
最后，这是来自犹他州麦肯齐的一个发现。

355
00:18:38.410 --> 00:18:44.250
When Andy is flipping the lights on and off, he steps away from the light switch and one of them turns back on magically.
当安迪开关灯时，他从灯开关旁走开，其中一盏灯却神奇地又亮了。

356
00:18:46.090 --> 00:18:49.170
Well, Mackenzie, that's because those switches don't control the lights.
嗯，麦肯齐，那是因为那些开关并不控制灯。

357
00:18:49.270 --> 00:18:52.750
It was actually someone off camera working a light board.
其实是场外有人在操作灯光控制台。

358
00:18:52.970 --> 00:18:53.590
Good catch.
发现得好。

359
00:18:54.090 --> 00:19:01.310
Well, there is one saving moment for Andy in this cold open, which is that Stanley joins him in singing the song.
嗯，安迪在这个开场戏里有一个挽回形象的时刻，那就是斯坦利加入了他一起唱歌。

360
00:19:01.690 --> 00:19:03.850
And Leslie David Baker has a great voice.
而且莱斯利·大卫·贝克的嗓音很棒。

361
00:19:04.410 --> 00:19:06.610
I loved seeing this side of Stanley.
我喜欢看到斯坦利的这一面。

362
00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:08.010
I loved seeing him sing.
我喜欢看他唱歌。

363
00:19:08.450 --> 00:19:11.510
I mean, of course, Stanley doesn't care at all about this tradition.
我的意思是，当然，斯坦利根本不在乎这个传统。

364
00:19:11.690 --> 00:19:13.510
He just is happy that he gets to go home.
他只是很高兴自己能回家了。

365
00:19:13.690 --> 00:19:14.110
Exactly.
没错。

366
00:19:14.110 --> 00:19:17.730
But I thought that was such a nice, fun button for the end of the cold open.
但我认为这给开场戏的结尾画上了一个既美好又有趣的句号。

367
00:19:17.730 --> 00:19:20.550
And makes sense character-wise as well.
而且从角色性格上来说也很合理。

368
00:19:21.470 --> 00:19:24.730
The episode is going to start in the conference room.
这一集将从会议室开始。

369
00:19:25.130 --> 00:19:26.410
Andy in Robert, California.
安迪在和加州的罗伯特交谈。

370
00:19:26.670 --> 00:19:27.610
Oh, so good.
哦，太棒了。

371
00:19:27.770 --> 00:19:29.690
Robert, California, please don't stop talking.
加州的罗伯特，请不要停止说话。

372
00:19:29.870 --> 00:19:32.210
I just love every single word you say.
我喜欢你说的每一个字。

373
00:19:32.770 --> 00:19:34.930
And Andy's trying to make small talk.
安迪正试图找话题闲聊。

374
00:19:35.130 --> 00:19:36.910
He's chatting with him about the Iron Chef.
他正在和他聊《铁人料理》。

375
00:19:37.590 --> 00:19:40.330
Dwight comes in wanting to assert himself into this meeting.
德怀特进来，想要强行加入这次会议。

376
00:19:40.510 --> 00:19:42.570
And Robert, California is like, did you need something?
而加州的罗伯特就像是说，你有什么事吗？

377
00:19:43.270 --> 00:19:45.010
Basically, he's like, why are you here?
基本上，他就是那个意思，你为什么在这儿？

378
00:19:46.010 --> 00:19:55.130
And like what you said, that was a goal of Danny and the writers, immediately you see Dwight just desperately wanting validation.
就像你说的，那是丹尼和编剧们的一个目标，你立刻就能看到德怀特迫切地想要得到认可。

379
00:19:56.850 --> 00:19:59.630
And it's going to play out throughout the whole episode.
这一点将在整集中持续体现。

380
00:20:00.510 --> 00:20:08.730
Robert, California is going to explain to Andy that they have this ticketing software that is able to kind of track mistakes that are made.
加州的罗伯特向安迪解释说，他们有一种票务软件，能够追踪所犯的错误。

381
00:20:08.730 --> 00:20:11.630
And there have been quite a lot of mistakes lately.
而且最近错误相当多。

382
00:20:12.390 --> 00:20:17.830
Danny said that the writers had to educate themselves on ticketing software, which at the time was very new.
丹尼说编剧们不得不去了解票务软件，这在当时是非常新鲜的东西。

383
00:20:17.970 --> 00:20:22.910
And it seemed kind of weird that you could track things that people were doing on their computer.
而且能追踪人们在电脑上做的事情，这看起来有点奇怪。

384
00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:24.610
He said it's very commonplace now.
他说现在这已经非常普遍了。

385
00:20:25.090 --> 00:20:31.490
He said he's pretty sure Justin Spitzer had a family member who had used ticketing software in their job.
他说他很确定贾斯汀·斯皮策有个亲戚在工作中用过票务软件。

386
00:20:31.630 --> 00:20:33.850
And so they kept hitting them up for details.
所以他们一直向他们询问细节。

387
00:20:34.490 --> 00:20:34.750
Awesome.
太棒了。

388
00:20:35.290 --> 00:20:41.370
Danny said that was often the case because most of the writers had very little knowledge about the actual workings of a normal office.
丹尼说这种情况经常发生，因为大多数编剧对普通办公室的实际运作知之甚少。

389
00:20:41.370 --> 00:20:46.370
So they'd always have to ask someone's friend or sister or family member or whatever.
所以他们总是不得不去问某人的朋友、姐妹或家庭成员之类的。

390
00:20:47.130 --> 00:20:48.270
And that's so true.
这太真实了。

391
00:20:48.410 --> 00:20:55.390
I mean, that's how Greg were always coming to you and I, Angela, because as cast members, we had worked extensively in corporate America.
我的意思是，格雷格总是那样来找你和我，安吉拉，因为作为演员，我们曾在企业界广泛工作过。

392
00:20:55.390 --> 00:20:55.850
Yeah.
没错。

393
00:20:56.230 --> 00:21:03.170
Well, Robert points out a few accounting errors and Andy says, well, that's Tweedledee and Tweedledum, which are both Kevin.
嗯，罗伯特指出了几个会计错误，安迪说，嗯，那是“笨蛋甲”和“笨蛋乙”，指的都是凯文。

394
00:21:03.570 --> 00:21:04.050
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

395
00:21:04.370 --> 00:21:04.650
Yeah.
没错。

396
00:21:05.090 --> 00:21:10.110
And then Robert has a little speech here that's one of my favorites in the whole entire episode.
然后罗伯特在这里有一段小演讲，是我整集中最喜欢的片段之一。

397
00:21:10.110 --> 00:21:11.050
I have to read it.
我必须读一下。

398
00:21:11.390 --> 00:21:14.950
He basically says to Andy, end the mistakes.
他基本上对安迪说，停止这些错误。

399
00:21:15.490 --> 00:21:22.610
And then he says, when I come back next week and this printout shows me no mistakes, then we can talk about names all day.
然后他说，当我下周回来，这份打印件显示没有任何错误时，我们就可以整天讨论名字了。

400
00:21:22.790 --> 00:21:27.550
Our favorite names, silly made-up names, our normal names said in a silly voice.
我们最喜欢的名字，愚蠢的虚构名字，或者用滑稽的声音说出我们的正常名字。

401
00:21:27.730 --> 00:21:28.650
Wouldn't that be nice?
那不是很好吗？

402
00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:30.130
I love that, too.
我也很喜欢那个。

403
00:21:30.130 --> 00:21:32.270
I want to see that conversation.
我很想看看那段对话。

404
00:21:32.690 --> 00:21:39.190
I want to see him come back next week and really just let Andy go with the names and the nicknames.
我希望他下周能回来，真的让安迪随心所欲地起名字和绰号。

405
00:21:39.570 --> 00:21:39.970
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

406
00:21:40.570 --> 00:21:49.430
Well, Jim is going to bounce into Daryl's office and invite him and Justine to go on a double date with him and Pam to the roller derby.
吉姆打算冲进达里尔的办公室，邀请他和贾斯汀跟自己和帕姆一起去玩轮滑德比。

407
00:21:50.030 --> 00:21:51.890
I know, roller derby.
我知道，轮滑德比。

408
00:21:51.970 --> 00:21:52.990
I was so curious.
我当时特别好奇。

409
00:21:52.990 --> 00:21:53.830
I was like, okay.
我就想，好吧。

410
00:21:54.250 --> 00:21:56.250
Well, that's because of the NBA lockout.
嗯，那是因为NBA停摆了。

411
00:21:56.410 --> 00:21:58.590
They're looking to do some new stuff, I guess.
我想他们是想尝试些新东西。

412
00:21:58.930 --> 00:21:59.370
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

413
00:21:59.370 --> 00:22:01.870
We got a fan question from Sarah S.
我们收到了来自萨拉·S的一位粉丝提问。

414
00:22:01.970 --> 00:22:03.250
in Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania.
她来自宾夕法尼亚州的威尔克斯-巴里。

415
00:22:03.510 --> 00:22:04.090
Hi, Sarah.
你好，萨拉。

416
00:22:04.590 --> 00:22:12.510
Sarah says, I have been waiting for this episode to come up because of this scene when Jim talks to Daryl about going to see roller derby.
萨拉说，我一直等着这一集播出，就是因为吉姆跟达里尔谈论去看轮滑德比的那一幕。

417
00:22:12.730 --> 00:22:16.970
Well, they absolutely can see some roller derby near Scranton.
其实，他们绝对可以在斯克兰顿附近看到轮滑德比。

418
00:22:17.110 --> 00:22:25.590
I am part of the Wilkes-Barre-Scranton Roller Derby League, and at the time that this episode aired, our team would have existed and been skating in Wilkes-Barre.
我是威尔克斯-巴里-斯克兰顿轮滑德比联盟的一员，在这一集播出的时候，我们的队伍就已经存在，并且在威尔克斯-巴里滑冰了。

419
00:22:25.590 --> 00:22:34.870
Now, we practice and play in Musick right down the road from Terry Steiner, and you're more than welcome to drop in whenever you come for your Scranton tour.
现在，我们在离特里·施泰纳不远的穆西克训练和比赛，你们随时欢迎来参观，只要你们来斯克兰顿巡游的话。

420
00:22:35.570 --> 00:22:36.430
Signed, Sarah.
签名，萨拉。

421
00:22:36.810 --> 00:22:39.970
And then her roller derby name is Alexander Slamilton.
然后她的轮滑德比名字叫亚历山大·斯兰密尔顿。

422
00:22:41.070 --> 00:22:41.590
Slamilton!
斯兰密尔顿！

423
00:22:42.250 --> 00:22:42.730
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

424
00:22:43.110 --> 00:22:45.770
Sarah, I would love to come, and I'd love to bring my skates.
萨拉，我很乐意去，也很乐意带上我的溜冰鞋。

425
00:22:45.910 --> 00:22:49.910
Can I just go one lap around, like I don't, I don't, with no bumps?
我可以只滑一圈吗？就是那种，我不，我不，不跟人碰撞的那种？

426
00:22:51.770 --> 00:22:52.450
All right, listen.
好吧，听着。

427
00:22:52.590 --> 00:22:53.950
In this scene, I have a catch.
在这一幕里，我发现了一个小细节。

428
00:22:54.310 --> 00:22:54.770
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

429
00:22:54.770 --> 00:22:56.210
It's Daryl's mug.
是达里尔的杯子。

430
00:22:56.410 --> 00:23:01.230
I don't know if you noticed it, but it says, CNC, Condren, and Company.
不知道你们有没有注意到，上面写着CNC，康德伦公司。

431
00:23:02.150 --> 00:23:02.710
No.
没有。

432
00:23:02.910 --> 00:23:04.090
What does that stand for?
那代表什么？

433
00:23:04.370 --> 00:23:06.690
Listen to what this mug represents.
听听这个杯子代表了什么。

434
00:23:07.330 --> 00:23:09.070
It represents a company.
它代表了一家公司。

435
00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:20.710
They are a full-service advertising and public relations firm, and what they do is they produce and place advertising on TV, radio, billboards, print, internet, and viral media.
他们是一家提供全方位服务的广告和公关公司，他们的业务是在电视、广播、广告牌、印刷品、互联网和病毒式媒体上制作并投放广告。

436
00:23:20.710 --> 00:23:30.470
